<interrogationes unitas="lapifex_generare">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn a grammar into a parse table?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_grammaticam_legere lapifex_first_computare lapifex_collectio_construere lapifex_collectio_lalr_construere lapifex_tabulam_construere" via="lib/nuntium_schema.c">Read
      the STML grammar with lapifex_grammaticam_legere, run
      lapifex_first_computare, build the item sets with
      lapifex_collectio_construere (canonical LR(1)) or
      lapifex_collectio_lalr_construere (LALR(1)), and turn them into an
      ACTION/GOTO table with lapifex_tabulam_construere. Every step
      returns NIHIL or FALSUM on failure. lib/nuntium_schema.c is the
      live consumer and runs exactly this chain (it also calls
      lapifex_follow_computare) before handing the table to the
      lapifex parser.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does the STML grammar format look like?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_grammaticam_legere LapifexSymbolum" via="lib/nuntium_schema.c">A
      root with three required children: &lt;terminalia&gt; holding
      &lt;terminalis titulus=".." exemplum=".." genus=".."/&gt; (titulus
      required; exemplum is the literal or a $identificator/$numerus
      class used by the lexer; genus is optional), &lt;regulae&gt; holding
      &lt;regula titulus="lhs"&gt; with one &lt;productio&gt;A B C&lt;/productio&gt;
      per alternative (symbols separated by spaces), and
      &lt;initium&gt;start&lt;/initium&gt;. Every name in a production must
      be a declared terminal or a regula titulus.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I write an empty (epsilon) production?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_grammaticam_legere lapifex_first_computare">No.
      The reader skips a &lt;productio&gt; whose text is empty after
      trimming, silently, so an empty alternative simply disappears
      from the grammar. FIRST and FOLLOW do handle epsilon
      (habet_epsilon, productions with an empty right side), but no
      STML grammar can produce one. Write an optional part out as two
      alternatives instead: A -&gt; B C and A -&gt; C rather than
      A -&gt; opt_B C with an empty opt_B.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why is there a separate LALR(1) builder, and when should I use it?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_collectio_lalr_construere lapifex_collectio_construere" via="lib/lapifex_generare.worklog.md lib/lapifex_c89_grammatica.c">The
      canonical LR(1) builder makes a separate state for every
      lookahead combination and blew up on the C89 expression grammar
      (95 productions): it ran indefinitely. The LALR(1) builder makes
      the LR(0) automaton first (about 155 states there), propagates
      lookaheads to a fixed point, and returns the same
      LapifexCollectio type, so it is a drop-in replacement; that
      table took about two seconds. Small grammars such as
      nuntium_schema's still use the canonical builder.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what are __initium__, EOF and the -1 terminal?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_grammaticam_legere lapifex_tabulam_construere LapifexActioIntroitus">The
      reader adds an augmented non-terminal __initium__ with one last
      production: __initium__ -&gt; start EOF when a terminal titled
      exactly "EOF" is declared, otherwise __initium__ -&gt; start, and
      makes it the grammar's initium_index. Terminal index -1 is the
      end marker $ used as lookahead; ACCEPT is the action on -1 in the
      state where the augmented production is complete. A terminal
      named anything else ("END", "eof") gets no special
      treatment.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I know which production number my reduce callback receives?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_grammaticam_imprimere LapifexProductio" via="lib/nuntium_schema.c">Productions
      are numbered from 0 in document order: regula by regula,
      productio by productio (an empty &lt;productio&gt; is skipped and
      takes no number), with the augmented __initium__ production
      last. Symbols are numbered the same way: terminals first in
      declaration order, then the regula non-terminals, then
      __initium__. lapifex_grammaticam_imprimere prints both lists
      (P0, P1, ...), and nuntium_schema.c switches on exactly those
      numbers (its P15 is __initium__ -&gt; schema EOF).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I see the conflicts in my grammar?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_conflictus_imprimere lapifex_tabulam_imprimere lapifex_actiones_quaerere LapifexTabula">The
      table keeps ALL actions for a (state, terminal) pair, because
      the GLR parser forks on them: conflicting entries get
      conflictus_intentus VERUM, the state habet_conflictum, and
      LapifexTabula.numerus_conflictuum counts conflicting PAIRS (three
      actions on one terminal count as three). lapifex_conflictus_imprimere
      lists them on stdout, lapifex_tabulam_imprimere prints the whole
      table, and lapifex_actiones_quaerere returns every action for a
      pair.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep one table and parse many inputs with it?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_actiones_quaerere lapifex_goto_quaerere" via="lib/lapifex_c89_grammatica.c lib/lapifex_parsere.c">Yes,
      lib/lapifex_c89_grammatica.c builds its table once into a static
      piscina. But every call to lapifex_actiones_quaerere allocates a
      NEW Xar from the grammar's piscina (tabula-&gt;grammatica-&gt;piscina)
      for its result, and the parsers call it once per step, so that
      piscina grows with every token parsed for the life of the table.
      lapifex_goto_quaerere allocates nothing.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does lapifex_grammaticam_legere do with a bad grammar?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_grammaticam_legere">It
      prints a message to stderr ("lapifex: ...") and returns NIHIL for
      unparsable STML, a missing &lt;terminalia&gt;, &lt;regulae&gt; or
      &lt;initium&gt;, a terminalis or regula without titulus, or a
      production or initium naming an unknown symbol. It does NOT
      reject a regula whose titulus equals a terminal's: the
      productions are attached to the terminal as their left side,
      silently; likewise two regulae with the same titulus are merged
      into one non-terminal.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I build the table without computing FOLLOW sets?
    <responsum symbolum="lapifex_follow_computare lapifex_first_computare lapifex_collectio_construere">Yes.
      Both collection builders use only FIRST (they compute
      FIRST(beta a) for the lookaheads), so lapifex_first_computare is
      required and lapifex_follow_computare is not; FOLLOW sets are only
      read by lapifex_follow_obtinere and lapifex_follow_imprimere. Both
      sets are Xar of s32 terminal indices (the header comment on
      lapifex_first_obtinere says i32; the elements are s32). The end marker -1 appears
      only in FOLLOW sets (lapifex_follow_computare seeds it into the
      start symbol's FOLLOW); a symbol's FIRST set holds real terminal
      indices only.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
